__d(function(e,a,t,n,o,s,r){o.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:7,subject:"Mathematics",textbook:"Mathematics \u2013 Class 7",chapter_number:13,chapter_title:"Exponents and Powers",estimated_study_time_minutes:50,difficulty:"Easy",learning_objectives:["Understand what an exponent (power) is and write repeated multiplication in exponential form","Identify the base and the exponent in any exponential expression","Evaluate expressions of the form a^m for whole number bases and exponents","Apply the laws of exponents: product rule, quotient rule, power of a power, zero exponent","Express very large numbers using powers of 10 (scientific notation basics)","Compare and simplify expressions using laws of exponents","Solve word problems involving exponents and powers","Use exponents to write prime factorisation in index form"],prerequisite_knowledge:["Multiplication of whole numbers and integers","Prime factorisation from earlier classes","Basic understanding of multiplication as repeated addition"],introduction:{why_important:"Exponents let us write very large or very small numbers compactly. The distance from Earth to the Sun is about 150,000,000 km \u2014 writing this as 1.5 \xd7 10^8 is far easier. Scientists, engineers, and computer scientists use exponents every day to handle numbers that would otherwise fill an entire page.",real_life_relevance:"When your phone says it has 128 GB of storage, that is 128 \xd7 2^30 bytes. When a bacteria colony doubles every hour, after 10 hours it has grown by 2^10 = 1024 times. Bank interest, population growth, and earthquake magnitude (Richter scale) all use exponential calculations.",karnataka_context:"The Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO) headquartered in Bengaluru uses powers of 10 constantly \u2014 the distance to the Moon is about 3.84 \xd7 10^5 km, and satellite orbital calculations involve numbers in exponential form. Karnataka's IT industry in Bengaluru (called the Silicon Valley of India) stores data in units of 2^10 (kilo), 2^20 (mega), 2^30 (giga) \u2014 all powers of 2.",connection_to_previous_learning:"In earlier chapters you multiplied integers and worked with prime factorisation. This chapter extends multiplication \u2014 instead of writing 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 2, you write 2^5. Exponents are a shortcut for repeated multiplication."},core_concepts:[{concept_name:"Base, Exponent, and Exponential Form",simple_explanation:"When we multiply a number by itself many times, we can write it as a power. For example, 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 2 = 2\xb3. Here 2 is the base (the number being multiplied) and 3 is the exponent (how many times it is multiplied).",detailed_explanation:"The expression a\u1d50 is read as 'a to the power m' or 'a raised to m'. Here 'a' is the BASE and 'm' is the EXPONENT or INDEX. The value a\u1d50 = a \xd7 a \xd7 a \xd7 \u2026 (m times). For example: 5\xb3 = 5 \xd7 5 \xd7 5 = 125. 2\u2076 = 64. Any number to the power 1 equals itself: a\xb9 = a. Any number to the power 0 equals 1: a\u2070 = 1 (where a \u2260 0). Negative bases follow sign rules: (\u22122)\xb3 = \u22128 (odd power \u2192 negative), (\u22122)\u2074 = 16 (even power \u2192 positive). We also use exponents to write prime factorisation: 360 = 2\xb3 \xd7 3\xb2 \xd7 5.",real_life_example:"A chess board legend says a grain of rice was doubled on each square: square 1 = 2\u2070 = 1 grain, square 2 = 2\xb9 = 2, square 3 = 2\xb2 = 4, \u2026 square 64 = 2\u2076\xb3 grains. That final square alone holds more rice than the world has ever produced \u2014 showing how quickly exponential growth works.",karnataka_example:"ISRO's Chandrayaan-3 landed on the Moon in 2023. The Moon is about 384,000 km from Earth = 3.84 \xd7 10\u2075 km. The mass of the spacecraft was about 3,900 kg \u2248 3.9 \xd7 10\xb3 kg. Both of these are written using powers of 10 \u2014 a direct application of exponential form used by ISRO scientists in Bengaluru every day.",common_mistakes:"Students often confuse the base and exponent. In 2\xb3, the 3 is the EXPONENT (not the base). Also, students write 2\xb3 = 6 (multiplying instead of using exponent). Correct: 2\xb3 = 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 2 = 8, not 2 \xd7 3 = 6. Another error: (\u22123)\xb2 = 9 (positive, even power) \u2260 \u22129.",memory_tip:"BASE is at the BOTTOM (like a building's base). EXPONENT is up HIGH (like a flag on top). a\u1d50: a is the BASE (below), m is the EXPONENT (raised up). 'Exponent EXPONENTIALLY raises the value'!"},{concept_name:"Laws of Exponents",simple_explanation:"There are easy rules for multiplying, dividing, and raising powers. These rules save time when working with exponents and are used across all of science and mathematics.",detailed_explanation:"The five main laws of exponents (where a, b are non-zero and m, n are whole numbers):\n1. Product Rule: a\u1d50 \xd7 a\u207f = a\u1d50\u207a\u207f (same base \u2192 add exponents). E.g., 2\xb3 \xd7 2\u2074 = 2\u2077.\n2. Quotient Rule: a\u1d50 \xf7 a\u207f = a\u1d50\u207b\u207f (same base \u2192 subtract exponents). E.g., 3\u2075 \xf7 3\xb2 = 3\xb3.\n3. Power of a Power: (a\u1d50)\u207f = a\u1d50\u02e3\u207f (multiply the exponents). E.g., (2\xb3)\u2074 = 2\xb9\xb2.\n4. Product of Powers: (ab)\u1d50 = a\u1d50 \xd7 b\u1d50. E.g., (2\xd73)\u2074 = 2\u2074 \xd7 3\u2074.\n5. Zero Exponent: a\u2070 = 1 for any a \u2260 0. E.g., 7\u2070 = 1, 100\u2070 = 1.\nThese laws only apply when bases are THE SAME (for rules 1 and 2).",real_life_example:"When a computer doubles its processing speed: if it doubles 3 times (2\xb3) and then doubles 4 more times (2\u2074), the total increase is 2\xb3 \xd7 2\u2074 = 2\u2077 = 128 times \u2014 much easier to calculate using the product rule than multiplying out all 128 doublings separately.",karnataka_example:"In Karnataka's IT sector, data storage uses powers of 2: 1 KB = 2\xb9\u2070 bytes, 1 MB = 2\xb2\u2070 bytes = (2\xb9\u2070)\xb2 bytes. This uses the Power of a Power rule: (2\xb9\u2070)\xb2 = 2\xb2\u2070. When Infosys or Wipro (both headquartered in Bengaluru) calculate data storage needs, these exponential laws underpin every calculation.",common_mistakes:"Most common mistake: applying the product rule to DIFFERENT bases. 2\xb3 \xd7 3\u2074 CANNOT be simplified to 2\u2077 or 3\u2077 \u2014 the bases must be THE SAME. Another error: (2\xb3)\u2074 = 2\u2077 (students add instead of multiply). Correct: (2\xb3)\u2074 = 2\xb9\xb2. Also: 2\xb3 + 2\u2074 \u2260 2\u2077 \u2014 laws of exponents only apply to MULTIPLICATION and DIVISION, not addition.",memory_tip:"Product Rule: SAME base + MULTIPLY \u2192 ADD exponents (\xd7\u2192+). Quotient Rule: SAME base + DIVIDE \u2192 SUBTRACT exponents (\xf7\u2192\u2212). Power of Power: POWER on POWER \u2192 MULTIPLY exponents (()\u2192\xd7). Remember: operations on exponents are one step DOWN from operations on values!"},{concept_name:"Expressing Large Numbers Using Powers of 10",simple_explanation:"Very large numbers (like the distance to stars, population of India, or the number of cells in a body) can be written neatly using powers of 10. For example, 1,000,000 = 10\u2076.",detailed_explanation:"Powers of 10 form the basis of our number system: 10\u2070=1, 10\xb9=10, 10\xb2=100, 10\xb3=1000, 10\u2074=10,000, 10\u2075=1,00,000, 10\u2076=10,00,000 etc. Any large number can be expressed using powers of 10. For example: India's population \u2248 1,40,00,00,000 = 1.4 \xd7 10\u2079. Distance from Earth to Sun \u2248 15,00,00,000 km = 1.5 \xd7 10\u2078 km. Speed of light \u2248 3,00,000 km/s = 3 \xd7 10\u2075 km/s. The standard form: a \xd7 10\u207f where 1 \u2264 a < 10 is called Scientific Notation (introduced at this level). Expanded form using powers of 10: 47,561 = 4\xd710\u2074 + 7\xd710\xb3 + 5\xd710\xb2 + 6\xd710\xb9 + 1\xd710\u2070.",real_life_example:"The human body has about 37,000,000,000,000 (37 trillion) cells = 3.7 \xd7 10\xb9\xb3 cells. Writing it as 3.7 \xd7 10\xb9\xb3 is far neater than 37,000,000,000,000. Scientists ALWAYS use this compact form \u2014 it is one of the most practical applications of exponents.",karnataka_example:"Karnataka has a land area of about 1,91,791 km\xb2 \u2248 1.9 \xd7 10\u2075 km\xb2. The population of Bengaluru city is about 1,30,00,000 = 1.3 \xd7 10\u2077. India's GDP in 2024 was about \u20b93,00,00,000 crore = \u20b93 \xd7 10\u2077 crore. All these Karnataka and India-specific numbers are naturally expressed using powers of 10.",common_mistakes:"Students sometimes write 2,500 as 25 \xd7 10\xb2 (correct mathematically but not in standard form \u2014 standard form requires the first factor to be between 1 and 10). Correct standard form: 2.5 \xd7 10\xb3. Also, students confuse 10\xb3 = 1000 with 10\xb3 = 300 (thinking 10\xd73=30). Remember: 10\xb3 = 10\xd710\xd710 = 1000, not 10\xd73.",memory_tip:"Count the zeros: 10\u2076 = 1 followed by 6 zeros = 1,000,000. The EXPONENT tells you HOW MANY ZEROS follow the 1. Powers of 10 = zero-counting machine! 10^n = 1 with n zeros."}],key_terms_glossary:[{term:"Exponent",meaning:"The number that tells how many times the base is multiplied by itself. In 2\u2075, the exponent is 5. Also called 'index' or 'power'."},{term:"Base",meaning:"The number being repeatedly multiplied. In 2\u2075, the base is 2. The expression means 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 2 = 32."},{term:"Power",meaning:"The entire expression a\u1d50 is called a power. Also used as a synonym for exponent. '2 to the power 5' means 2\u2075."},{term:"Exponential form",meaning:"Writing a number using a base and an exponent. E.g., 8 = 2\xb3 is the exponential form of 8."},{term:"Product Rule",meaning:"When multiplying two powers with the SAME base, add the exponents: a\u1d50 \xd7 a\u207f = a\u1d50\u207a\u207f. E.g., 3\u2074 \xd7 3\xb2 = 3\u2076."},{term:"Quotient Rule",meaning:"When dividing two powers with the SAME base, subtract the exponents: a\u1d50 \xf7 a\u207f = a\u1d50\u207b\u207f. E.g., 5\u2076 \xf7 5\xb2 = 5\u2074."},{term:"Zero Exponent",meaning:"Any non-zero number raised to the power 0 equals 1. a\u2070 = 1 (a \u2260 0). E.g., 7\u2070 = 1, 100\u2070 = 1, (\u22123)\u2070 = 1."},{term:"Power of a Power",meaning:"When raising a power to another power, multiply the exponents: (a\u1d50)\u207f = a\u1d50\u207f. E.g., (2\xb3)\u2074 = 2\xb9\xb2."},{term:"Standard form",meaning:"Writing a number as a \xd7 10\u207f where 1 \u2264 a < 10. E.g., 47,000 = 4.7 \xd7 10\u2074. Also called scientific notation."},{term:"Prime factorisation in index form",meaning:"Writing a number's prime factors using exponents. E.g., 72 = 2\xb3 \xd7 3\xb2. This is the most compact way to express prime factorisation."}],worked_examples:[{example_number:1,type:"Comprehension",question:"Simplify: 3\u2074 \xd7 3\xb2 \xf7 3\xb3",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Identify \u2014 all bases are 3 (same base), so laws of exponents apply.","Step 2: Apply product rule first: 3\u2074 \xd7 3\xb2 = 3\u2074\u207a\xb2 = 3\u2076.","Step 3: Apply quotient rule: 3\u2076 \xf7 3\xb3 = 3\u2076\u207b\xb3 = 3\xb3.","Step 4: Evaluate: 3\xb3 = 3 \xd7 3 \xd7 3 = 27.","Conclusion: 3\u2074 \xd7 3\xb2 \xf7 3\xb3 = 27."]},{example_number:2,type:"Vocabulary in context",question:"Express 1,25,000 in standard form (using powers of 10).",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Write the number \u2014 1,25,000.","Step 2: Place decimal after first digit: 1.25000.","Step 3: Count how many places the decimal moved left: 5 places.","Step 4: Write in standard form: 1.25 \xd7 10\u2075.","Step 5: Verify: 1.25 \xd7 10\u2075 = 1.25 \xd7 1,00,000 = 1,25,000 \u2713","Conclusion: 1,25,000 = 1.25 \xd7 10\u2075."]},{example_number:3,type:"Moral application",question:"A bacteria colony starts with 1 cell and doubles every hour. How many cells after 8 hours? Express using exponents.",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Identify pattern \u2014 doubles each hour means multiply by 2 each time.","Step 2: After 1 hour: 2\xb9 = 2 cells. After 2 hours: 2\xb2 = 4 cells.","Step 3: After 8 hours: 2\u2078 cells.","Step 4: Evaluate: 2\u2078 = 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 2 = 256 cells.","Conclusion: After 8 hours, there are 2\u2078 = 256 cells. Exponents perfectly model exponential growth \u2014 one of the most important patterns in biology, finance, and technology."]}],quick_check_questions:[{after_concept:"Laws of Exponents",questions:[{type:"Recall",q:"What is 5\u2070?",a:"5\u2070 = 1. Any non-zero number raised to the power 0 equals 1."},{type:"Recall",q:"Simplify 2\u2074 \xd7 2\xb3 using the product rule.",a:"2\u2074 \xd7 2\xb3 = 2\u2074\u207a\xb3 = 2\u2077 = 128."},{type:"Understanding",q:"Can you simplify 3\xb2 \xd7 4\xb3 using the product rule? Why or why not?",a:"No. The product rule (a\u1d50 \xd7 a\u207f = a\u1d50\u207a\u207f) only applies when the BASES ARE THE SAME. Here the bases are 3 and 4 \u2014 different bases. So 3\xb2 \xd7 4\xb3 cannot be simplified using this rule."},{type:"Understanding",q:"Simplify (5\xb2)\xb3.",a:"(5\xb2)\xb3 = 5\xb2\u02e3\xb3 = 5\u2076 = 15,625. Using the power of a power rule: multiply the exponents."},{type:"Application",q:"Write 3,60,00,000 in standard form.",a:"3,60,00,000 = 3.6 \xd7 10\u2077. (Decimal moves 7 places left from after the 3.)"}]}],key_takeaways:["a\u1d50 means a multiplied by itself m times. 'a' is the base; 'm' is the exponent/index.","Any number to the power 0 is 1: a\u2070 = 1 (a \u2260 0).","Any number to the power 1 is itself: a\xb9 = a.","Product rule: a\u1d50 \xd7 a\u207f = a\u1d50\u207a\u207f \u2014 add exponents (same base only).","Quotient rule: a\u1d50 \xf7 a\u207f = a\u1d50\u207b\u207f \u2014 subtract exponents (same base only).","Power of a power: (a\u1d50)\u207f = a\u1d50\u207f \u2014 multiply the exponents.","Negative base to even power = positive; negative base to odd power = negative.","Standard form: a \xd7 10\u207f where 1 \u2264 a < 10 \u2014 used for very large/small numbers.","Prime factorisation in index form: 360 = 2\xb3 \xd7 3\xb2 \xd7 5.","Karnataka link: ISRO (Bengaluru) uses powers of 10 for space distances; IT sector uses powers of 2 for data storage."],mind_map:{root:"Exponents and Powers",branches:[{branch:"Basic Concepts",sub:["Base and exponent","a\u1d50 = a \xd7 a \xd7 \u2026 (m times)","Exponential form","a\u2070 = 1, a\xb9 = a"]},{branch:"Laws of Exponents",sub:["Product: a\u1d50\xd7a\u207f = a\u1d50\u207a\u207f","Quotient: a\u1d50\xf7a\u207f = a\u1d50\u207b\u207f","Power of power: (a\u1d50)\u207f = a\u1d50\u207f","Same base rule!"]},{branch:"Signs with Exponents",sub:["Even power \u2192 always positive","Odd power \u2192 keeps sign","(\u22123)\xb2 = 9, (\u22123)\xb3 = \u221227"]},{branch:"Large Numbers",sub:["Standard form: a \xd7 10\u207f","Powers of 10","Prime factorisation in index form"]},{branch:"Applications",sub:["ISRO space distances","IT data storage (powers of 2)","Population and growth","Compound interest"]}]},revision_notes:"Exponents and Powers introduces the shorthand a\u1d50 for repeated multiplication of a by itself m times. Key vocabulary: base (a), exponent/index (m), power (the full expression a\u1d50). Evaluation: 2\u2075 = 32, 3\xb3 = 27, 10\u2074 = 10,000. Special cases: a\u2070 = 1 (any non-zero base), a\xb9 = a. Negative bases: even exponent \u2192 positive result; odd exponent \u2192 negative result. Five laws: (1) Product rule a\u1d50 \xd7 a\u207f = a\u1d50\u207a\u207f (same base, add exponents); (2) Quotient rule a\u1d50 \xf7 a\u207f = a\u1d50\u207b\u207f (same base, subtract); (3) Power of a power (a\u1d50)\u207f = a\u1d50\u207f (multiply exponents); (4) (ab)\u1d50 = a\u1d50b\u1d50; (5) a\u2070 = 1. CRITICAL: laws 1 and 2 require the SAME base. Standard form: a \xd7 10\u207f where 1 \u2264 a < 10. Example: 3,75,000 = 3.75 \xd7 10\u2075. Prime factorisation in index form: 72 = 2\xb3 \xd7 3\xb2. Karnataka connection: ISRO uses 10\u207f for space calculations; Bengaluru's IT sector uses 2\u207f for data storage.",must_remember_facts:["a\u1d50: a = base, m = exponent. Read as 'a to the power m'.","a\u2070 = 1 for any a \u2260 0. This is a LAW, not a coincidence.","Product rule: a\u1d50 \xd7 a\u207f = a\u1d50\u207a\u207f (SAME base \u2192 ADD exponents).","Quotient rule: a\u1d50 \xf7 a\u207f = a\u1d50\u207b\u207f (SAME base \u2192 SUBTRACT exponents).","Power of power: (a\u1d50)\u207f = a\u1d50\u207f (MULTIPLY the exponents).","2\xb3 = 8, 2\u2074 = 16, 2\u2075 = 32, 2\xb9\u2070 = 1024 \u2014 memorise these.","(\u2212a)\u1d49\u1d5b\u1d49\u207f = positive; (\u2212a)\u1d52\u1d48\u1d48 = negative.","Standard form: between 1 and 10, multiplied by a power of 10.","10\xb3 = 1000 (3 zeros), 10\u2076 = 10,00,000 (6 zeros).","Laws of exponents apply to MULTIPLICATION/DIVISION \u2014 NOT to addition/subtraction."]}},2597,[]);